## ABSTRACT
Lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been rapidly developed in the past decade. Owing to its excellent power conversion efficiency with robust and low-cost fabrication, perovskite quickly becomes one of the most promising candidates for the next-generation photovoltaic technology. With the development of PSCs, the interface engineering has witnessed its increasingly critical role in maximizing the device performance as well as the long-term stability, because the interfaces in PSCs are closely correlated with the defect management, carrier dynamics and surface passivation. This review focuses on interfacial modification between the perovskite active layer and the charge transport layer, as well as the recent advances on high-efficiency and stable PSCs driven by interface engineering strategies. The contributing roles of interface engineering in terms of defect passivation, inhibiting ion migration, optimization of energy band alignment and morphological control are discussed. Finally, based on the latest progress and advances, strategies and opportunities for the future research on interface engineering for PSCs are proposed to promote the development of perovskite photovoltaic technology.
## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cells, interface engineering, defects passivation, band-level alignment
## 1 Introduction
of the PSCs with reported PCE over \(21\%\) have been achieved with interface engineering. On one hand, due to the interface defects, energy band alignment and charge distribution, the interface has a significant influence on the photoelectric performance of the device [26–31]. On the other hand, due to its impact on the ion migration and resulted material degradation, the interface will also affect the stability of the devices [32–34]. Interface engineering is therefore designed to tackle with these problems by modifying the interface characteristics or introducing additional interlayers. At present, most of the studies on the interface modification have been focused on the electron transporting layer (ETL)/perovskite and perovskite/hole
transporting layer (HTL) interfaces, because these two interfaces are closely related to the extraction of photo-generated carriers in the perovskite layer [35–38]. which is essential in improving the device performance of PSCs. Additionally, proper modification of the exterior interfaces between the perovskite layer and the charge-transport layer above can effectively protect the perovskite absorber against the performance degradation initiated by moisture and oxygen [39–41], and thereby improve the device stability [42, 43]. The modification of the interior interface between the perovskite layer and the underneath charge-transport layer is helpful to improve the crystallinity and film quality of the perovskite absorber layer [44–46], resulting in perovskite films with larger grains, fewer pinholes and uniform distribution, which is of great benefit for improving the device performance [47, 48].
As pointed out in the previous report [49], to push PCE of perovskite photovoltaics (up to \(25.5\%\) at present) towards its Shockley–Queisser theoretical limit, the key strategies lie in optimizing the growth of the perovskite, passivating its defects (surfaces, grain interiors and grain boundaries), adjusting the band-gap of perovskite materials, which are the problems that the interface modification approaches have proven to be effective. In the following sections, we review the recent progress on the interfacial modification between the perovskite layer and its adjacent charge transport layers, highlighting the effects of these modifications on defect passivation, energy level optimization, morphology control, and stability improvement. Finally, we conclude the review and perspectives for the future development direction of interface engineering of the PSCs.
## 2 ETL/Perovskite interface modification
The interface between perovskite and ETL is essential to the device performance of PSCs due to its strong impact on the electron extraction as well as the current-voltage \((I{-}V)\) hysteresis of the devices. Modifications on ETL/perovskite interface are mainly targeted at the passivation of surface defects of the perovskite layer, inhibiting ion migration and optimization of interfacial energy-band alignment to reduce the non-radiative recombination at the interface and maximize the open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{OC}})\) . Moreover, for n-i-p structure devices, the interface modification also plays a key role in the morphology control of the perovskite film, which is critical for achieving decent device performance and durability [50–54].
## 2.1 Defect passivation
It's generally considered that the deep level defects are the major cause for undesirable charge transfer and voltage loss, whilst shallow level defects contribute less to the deterioration of the device performance [55]. Therefore, reducing detrimental deep level defects is an effective way to improve the photovoltaic performance and operational stability of the device. For n-i-p structured devices, the modification materials are initially considered to passivate the defects existing or possibly generating in the inorganic ETL, thus providing a low defect density interface and improving the photoelectric performance of devices. At the early stage, fullerenes and their derivatives were firstly studied as ETL modification candidates because of its superior electron mobility and limited defect density. The self-assembled fullerene monolayer \(\mathrm{\DeltaC_{60}}\) -SAM) [56], [6,6]- phenyl- \(C_{61}\) -butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) [57] and other fullerene derivatives (e.g., [6,6]-phenyl- \(C_{61}\) -butyric acid-dioctyl\(3,3^{\prime}\) -(5-hydroxy-1,3-phenylene)-bis(2-cyanoacrylate) ester (PCBB2CN-2C8) [58]) on the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) can passivate the trap states, and thereby reducing the non-radiative recombination channels and improving electron extraction from the perovskite
It's generally considered that the deep level defects are the major cause for undesirable charge transfer and voltage loss, whilst shallow level defects contribute less to the deterioration of the device performance [55]. Therefore, reducing detrimental deep level defects is an effective way to improve the photovoltaic performance and operational stability of the device. For n-i-p structured devices, the modification materials are initially considered to passivate the defects existing or possibly generating in the inorganic ETL, thus providing a low defect density interface and improving the photoelectric performance of devices. At the early stage, fullerenes and their derivatives were firstly studied as ETL modification candidates because of its superior electron mobility and limited defect density. The self-assembled fullerene monolayer \(\mathrm{\DeltaC_{60}}\) -SAM) [56], [6,6]- phenyl- \(C_{61}\) -butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) [57] and other fullerene derivatives (e.g., [6,6]-phenyl- \(C_{61}\) -butyric acid-dioctyl\(3,3^{\prime}\) -(5-hydroxy-1,3-phenylene)-bis(2-cyanoacrylate) ester (PCBB2CN-2C8) [58]) on the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) can passivate the trap states, and thereby reducing the non-radiative recombination channels and improving electron extraction from the perovskite layer. Nevertheless, the complex synthesis process and photochemical instability of organic materials remains a huge concern for further applications. In contrast, inorganic modifiers have gradually become a research hotspot due to its stability, easy synthesis and low cost [59, 60]. Learning from silicon-based solar cells, the classic metal oxides with wide band gap has been introduced as interface passivation materials in PSCs. For instance, Liang et al. [61] introduced an ultrathin \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) interfacial layer between the mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (mp-TiO2) film and perovskite layer and obtained increased \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and PCE, which has been attributed to the inhibition of the formation of oxygen vacancies on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) surface under UV light and the reduced surface states at \(\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}(M A P b I_{3})}\) interfaces. The former significantly improves the work stability of the device, while the latter effectively reduces the carrier recombination rate by enhancing the electron extraction and injection property of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) interface (as shown in Fig. 2(a)). In comparison, the passivation strategy employing inorganic halogen salts in recent years are more popular. Zhu et al. [62] showed that photo-generated carrier recombination at the interface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{PVSK}\) is a key obstacle hindering the further improvement of typical PSC photovoltaic performance. Spin-coated \(\mathrm{NH_{4}F}\) on top of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was demonstrated to smooth the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film and effectively passivate interface defects, resulting in the reduced carrier recombination and enhanced electron extraction. Inspired by the effectiveness of the \(\mathrm{NH_{4}F}\) treatment, the mechanism was further elucidated by Jung et al. [63], where \(\mathrm{NH_{4}F}\) passivation was introduced for \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) based ETL films. Since the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer contains surface hydroxyl groups that generate trap states near the valence band, it is critical to reduce the defect sites on the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) surface. It was found that \(\mathrm{NH_{4}F}\) not only lead to reduced surface defects, but also induce chemical doping of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) simultaneously. The ammonium cation with weak acidity from \(\mathrm{NH_{4}F}\) can react with the terminal-hydroxyls \(\left(\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{T}}\right)\) on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) surface, forming ammonia gas and water vapor, and fluoride anions can be incorporated into the defect sites (as shown in Fig. 2(b)). PSCs with such treatment achieved photovoltaic performance of \(23.2\%\) at a higher voltage, much better than that of the control devices. On the other hand, the modification on ETL was also found effective to provide a favorable substrate in obtaining a perovskite films with low defect density. In particular, ionic liquids (IL) have been widely used for the ETL treatment due to their high ionic conductivity, high thermal stability and low volatility. In 2016, Yang et al. [64] reported 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate \([\mathrm{BMIM}]\mathrm{BF}_{4}\) IL modification at the ETL/ perovskite interface achieving a PCE of \(19.62\%\) , which surpassed the existing highest PCE for planar \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) based solar cells at that time. Due to the interaction between the \([\mathrm{BMIM}]^{+}\) group and the perovskite grains, the addition of IL provided a suitable environment to grow low trap-state density perovskite and provided a huge effect for the improvement of PSC performance. The same \([\mathrm{BMIM}]\mathrm{BF}_{4}\) IL was employed to modify the perovskite \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) interface by Neal et al. [65]. It was found that the perovskite deposited on the IL surface exhibits a lower defect density, which improved the charge-carrier lifetimes and mobilities, thereby effectively reducing non-radiative recombination. Interestingly, it was proposed that the crystallization of the perovskite on top of the IL prevents the formation of trap states at the perovskite/substrate interface, rather than defect passivation from a classical explanation.
ETL interface modification in p-i-h structured PSCs [66] is a little different, where the defects on the surface of perovskite films is the only focus since inorganic ETL are rarely used. The point defects on the surface of the perovskite can be generally divided into three categories: vacancy defects, interstitial defects,
and substitution defects [67]. For lead-based \(\mathrm{APbX}_{3}\) perovskite crystals, these point defects can be further divided into \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{A}}, \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Pb}}, \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{X}}\) (vacancy defects), \(\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{t}}, \mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{i}}, \mathrm{X}_{\mathrm{i}}\) (interstitial defects), \(\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{Pb}}, \mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{A}}, \mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{X}}, \mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{X}}, \mathrm{X}_{\mathrm{A}}, \mathrm{X}_{\mathrm{Pb}}\) (substitution defects) (as shown in Fig. 2(c)). Due to the superior compatibility with fullerene derivatives that commonly used as ETL in p-i-n devices, polymer-based interface modification materials are intensively studied to optimize contact and passivate surface defects. Recently, a novel organic polymer containing an indacenodithiophene unit (IDTT4PDI) was introduced by Wang et al. [68] as a multifunctional surface-modification layer to solve this issue. Both X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (as shown in Fig. 2(d)) and photoluminescence (PL) spectra (as shown in Fig. 2(e)) verified the weak coordination between the spin-coated IDTT4PDI and the uncoordinated lead ions on the perovskite surface, passivating \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{X}}\) vacancy defects and improving interface contact. Zhang et al. [69] demonstrated that poly(9-vinylcarbazole) (PVK) can induce the formation of a quasi-continuous flake perovskite film on the surface of the perovskite layer, thereby greatly reducing the degradation reaction caused by interface defects and exposed grain boundaries. Clearly, not only the performance enhancement, but also the stability of the modified interface should be considered to meet the requirements of the future commercialization. Luo et al. [70] reported a more n-type perovskite films on the surface of perovskite by a controlled secondary growth with guanidinium bromide. Such gradient perovskite films lead to a larger fraction of occupied versus vacant traps and a reduction in the rate of trap-assisted recombination. With effective trap passivation, the charge transfer between the perovskite film and ETL are significantly improved, obtaining a high \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of 1.21V without sacrificing photocurrent.
## 2.2 Inhibiting ion migration
As early as 2015, researchers have found some evidences of mobile ions in perovskite film [71, 72], and also proved that \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{MA}^{+}\)can migrate in perovskite film under electric field
[73–75], which has been considered as a major reason that lead to the photocurrent-voltage \((I-V)\) hysteresis. From the study of the effect of spraying deposition cycles of the compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer on the device hysteresis, it is confirmed by Rong et al. [76] that \(I-V\) hysteresis is related to the characteristics of ETL/perovskite interface, where the charge accumulation and recombination leads to more serious hysteresis. It was also proved by many literatures that the modification on ETL/ perovskite interface plays a vital role in suppressing the \(I-V\) hysteresis of PSCs [57, 77–80]. Inorganic materials are usually considered at the first place to modify the ETL/perovskite interface. One of our previous work investigated KCl as a modification layer [81]. We found that the relative intensity of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) peaks gradually increased as a function of KCl concentration (or KCl thickness). The low defect density interface caused by the synergistic effect of excessive \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and KCl can effectively inhibit the ion migration during operation, thus greatly reducing the device \(I-V\) hysteresis from \(35.3\%\) to 0 (as shown in Fig. 3(a)), which demonstrate the huge potential of the cost effective inorganic modification layer for PSCs. In the meantime, Huang et al. [82] demonstrated slot-die printed \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films for high PCE and large area flexible PSC modules. \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films were passivated by different potassium compounds, the results showed that \(\mathrm{K}^{+}\)may easily form KBr by substitutional reaction with \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\)to passivate the halide vacancies. A significant misalignment of the non-passivated perovskite films shows more moveable ions compared with the passivate perovskite films, resulting in higher hysteresis. Finally, a high-efficiency and hysteresis-free flexible PSC was realized, and the large-scale \((5\ \mathrm{cm}\times6\ \mathrm{cm})\) flexible modules obtained a PCE over \(15\%\). Inorganic metal sulfide materials such as \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\) was also studied as a modification layer to the ETL/perovskite interface to reduce hysteresis of the device (as shown in Fig. 3(b)) [83]. Due to the match of energy levels and decreased electron trap state densities of ETL films, \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\)-based interface engineering promotes the charge separation, transport and collection. Furthermore, CsSe/ZnS quantum dots (QD) composed of
binary inorganic materials were also employed to be inserted at the perovskite/ \( \mathrm{C}_{60} \) interface [84]. The hysteresis of the \( I{-}V \) scans found effectively suppressed where \( \mathrm{Se}^{2-} \) anion inhibited the diffusion of \( \Gamma \) toward the cathode (as shown in Fig. 3(c)). Besides, monolayer graphene has been also studied by Tavakoli et al. [85] to modify the \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) /perovskite interface. The hysteresis index is reduced from 6.4 to 1.39 (as shown in Fig. 3(d)), which is considered as a result of the improved carrier extraction. It is noted that the modification of fullerenes-based materials can be also used to suppress \( I{-}V \) hysteresis. Three fullerene derivatives (bis(phenethyl alcohol) malonate (FMPE-I), bis(ethylenediamine) malonamide (FEDA-I), bis(propane diamine) malonamide (FPDA-I)) were used to modify the perovskite/PCBM interface by Zheng et al. [86], demonstrating almost no hysteresis(as shown in Fig. 3(e)). Dong et al. [87] also found that the modification of fullerene derivatives is effective in reducing hysteresis for inorganic ETLs (as shown in Figs. 3(f) and 3(g)). More interestingly, the fullerene derivative can be blended with insulating polymers to be used as a modification to the ETL/perovskite interface. As reported by Peng et al. [88], where polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) has shown an excellent effect of reducing hysteresis when used as an interface layer alone because of its inhibition of ion migration and interface coincidence, but as an insulating material, the fill factor (FF) of the device will be at a lower value (as shown in Fig. 3(h)). Therefore, they added PCBM to PMMA to adjust the conductivity of the interface modification layer, and finally obtained a
high-efficiency device with negligible \( I{-}V \) hysteresis (as shown in Fig. 3(i)). Hu et al. [89] also confirmed the effect of this mixed modification layer. Furthermore, in order to clarify the role of PCBM in suppressing \( I{-}V \) hysteresis, Zhong et al. [90] studied the internal ion movement of the device by spin-coating PCBM and PCBM-grafted polymer (PPCBM) as the intermediate layer. It is interesting to note that although the hysteresis of the PCBM combined device is significantly reduced because of inhibited ion migration (as shown in Figs. 3(j)–3(k)), the \( I{-}V \) hysteresis of the device becomes larger again when PPCBM replaces the PCBM (as shown in Fig. 3(l)). This phenomenon of hysteresis is attributed to PCBM molecules are bound to a polymer backbone and therefore immobilized within the polymer layer, which indicates that the diffusion of PCBM molecules into the perovskite layer is crucial to the passivating effect towards ionic migration.
Apart from \( I{-}V \) hysteresis, ion migration also induces excess defects in the film [72, 75] and reduces the photostability of halide perovskites [91]. Moreover, ion-accumulation-induced interfacial band doping and band bending is also associated with mobile ions which require interfacial modification to make it under control [72, 92]. Fang et al. [93] introduced chemically crosslinked trimethylpropane triacrylate (TMTA) at perovskite/PCBM interface to suppress the ions diffusion toward electrode or migration along grain boundaries. The control device exhibits obviously broadened distribution of both \( \Gamma^{-} \)and \( \mathrm{Cu} \) after MPP tracking due to severe diffusion.
While with TMTA, \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)distribution overlaped well with Pb element and the boundary line of \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{Cu}\) can be also clearly observed (as shown in Fig. 4(a)). As a result, the TMTA modified device maintaining an initial PCE of \(80\%\) after \(1{,}000\,\mathrm{h}\) of MPP tracking under continuous illumination, demonstrating a decent long-term operational stability of their p-i-n structured PSCs. Another example is Y6, a hot non-fullerene acceptor materials in organic photovoltaics, which has been utilize a multifunctional interface layer for PSCs by Russell et al. [94]. From the time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (ToF-SIMS) data, a \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)peak signal was seen in the PCBM/Ag interface (as shown in Fig. 4(b)), whereas no \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)accumulation was found in the Y6/Ag interface (as shown in Fig. 4(c)), indicating an effective inhibition of ion migration throughout the device. He et al. [95] demonstrated that an interfacial management strategy using a boron chloride subphthalocyanine \(\left(\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\mathrm{SubPc}\right)\)/fullerene electron-transport layer simultaneously reduced the interfacial defect density and suppressed ion migration, which can be attributed to the unique properties of \(\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\mathrm{SubPc}\) by forming strong Pb-Cl bonds and the contribution of \(\mathrm{N}\) in \(\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\mathrm{SubPc}\) to \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)adsorption. Not only for small-area PSCs, the stability of PSC modules can also be enhanced by inhibiting \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)diffusion channels. Zero-dimensional (0D), one-dimensional (1D) and two-dimensional (2D) low-dimensional diffusion barriers (DBLs) were introduced by Han et al. [96] to block the diffusion channels and reduce undesired ions diffusion. Interestingly, ToF-SIMS showed that the extent of ion diffusion is found obviously reduced with the increase in the dimension of DBLs (as shown in Figs. 6(d)–4(f)). Eventually, the 2D-DBL realized the most stable (thermally stable at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for over \(1{,}000\,\mathrm{h}\)) and efficient PSC modules with an area of \(36\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\).
## 2.3 Morphology control
In n-i-p structure devices where the interface layer is introduced prior to the deposition of perovskite, the ETL/perovskite interface can affect the morphology of the perovskite film, such
as crystallinity, film coverage, and crystal orientation. To obtain uniform morphology and high crystallinity, \(C_{60}\) was early reported by Shahiduzzaman et al. [97] as an intermediate layer at the ETL/perovskite interface to change the surface characteristics, improving photocurrent and FF (as shown in Fig. 5(a)). After that, Cao et al. [98] reported that the interface modification of ETL with the fullerene derivative pyrrolidinyl fullerene \(C_{60}\) substituting phenol \(\left(\mathrm{NPC}_{60^{-}}\mathrm{OH}\right)\) leads to larger perovskite grains, lower density of pinholes, as well as smoother surface (as shown in Figs. 5(b) and 5(c)), which are collectively beneficial for the FF and PCE. The effects of the 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES) on the device performance were also investigated by Yang et al. [99]. The covered APTES layer was reported to increase the surface energy and affinity of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), inducing the formation of perovskite film with better coverage and crystallinity (as shown in Fig. 5(d)), which significantly improves PCE and reduces hysteresis of the device. At the same time, Li et al. [100] demonstrated that cesium chloride (CsCl) as a crystal seed can markedly enhance the coverage of the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Cl}_{x}\) absorber layer on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (as shown in Fig. 5(e)).
## 2.4 Band-level alignment
In addition to the functions described above, interface engineering has also been used as an effective strategy to improve energy-level alignment. Optimized energy level alignment at the ETL/perovskite interface has been credited for improving the electron transfer and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) either by inserting an interfacial layer or tuning the band alignment of ETL. Fullerenes and their derivatives are most frequently used band-level modifiers [80, 97, 101–103]. For instance, Tian et al. [104] developed a fullerene derivative 2,5-diphenyl \(C_{60}\) fulleropyrrolidine \(\left(\mathrm{DPC}_{60}\right)\) to alleviate its solubility in perovskite solvent. Because of its well-matched energy-band levels with perovskite layer (as shown in Fig. 6(a)) and high electron mobility, the energy loss in the electron transferring processes can be effectively reduced, and thus the electron extraction capability is significantly improved, leading
to a decent PCE of \(20.4\%\) compared to \(18.8\%\) of the control device. Bao et al. [105] reported a 3-dimethyl-2-phenyl-2,3-dihydro-1H-benzoimidazole (DMBI) doped PCBM interlayer to modify \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) ETL. Such modification significantly improved charge transfer at the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) interface benefiting from a much smaller energy level offset (as shown in Fig. 6(b)), leading to a prominent improvement in PCE of the devices from \(17.46\%\) to \(20.14\%\). The 6TIC-4F modifier was elucidated by Wang et al. [106], who employed the 6TIC-4F with a lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level of about \(-4.14\;\mathrm{eV}\), which sits between the conduction band minimum (CBM) of perovskite \((-3.49\,\mathrm{eV})\) and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) \((-4.25\,\mathrm{eV})\) (as shown in Fig. 6(c)). It enabled a better band alignment across the ETL/perovskite interface for improved electron extraction efficiency. Meanwhile, modifiers of the inorganic interfacial layer have also attracted research attention. \(\mathrm{Ba}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) was demonstrated effective in lifting the conduction band of meso- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) upward to a position closer to the perovskite energy level and reduce the optical band-gap (as shown in Fig. 6(d)), achieving more effective photo-generated electron transfer and lower recombination rate [107]. Another modifier should be noted is \(\mathrm{CaTiO}_{3}\), which is the "real" perovskite. As reported by Li et al. [108], the presence of \(\mathrm{CaTiO}_{3}\) is beneficial for preventing the charge recombination and promoting the electron extraction and transportation as a result of a more favorably matched energy level (as shown in Fig. 6(e)).
Different from the above, interfacial modification with alkaline metal halides may take effect by tuning the band level of ETL. Xie et al. [109] spin coated the rubidium bromide (RbBr) on the surface of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and found the RbBr modification has narrowed the bandgap of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) from 3.58 to \(3.34\;\mathrm{eV}\), therefore reducing the energy barrier at the ETL/perovskite interface and improve the electronic contact, contributing to a remarkable improvement in device performance. Some organic interfacial modification materials have also been reported to optimize the band level alignment by modifying the energy bands of ETL. A recent report by Shallcross et al. [110] systematically studied the perovskite layers on the surface of (3-aminopropyl)triethoxysilane APTES functionalized \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (as shown in Fig. 6(f)), which can enhance the nucleation of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) and reduce the energy barrier for electron transport and extraction. Poly[(9,9-bis(3'-(N,N-dimethylamino)propyl)-2,7-fluorene)-alt-2,7-(9,9-dioctylfluorene)] (PFN) and polystyrene (PS) were introduced by Zhuang et al. [111] as novel interface modification materials. Since the photoemission onset of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles film shifted toward a lower binding energy upon the modification and aligned well with CB of perovskite active layer, it effectively promotes the extraction and transportation of electrons (as shown in Fig. 6(g)). It is also worth noting that because of the layer by layer deposition, the organic modification materials must not be easily dissolved by the perovskite precursor, which significantly limits the available organic candidates and thus lead to fewer reports compared to the inorganic ETL/perovskite modification for n-i-p structured devices.
## 3 Perovskite/HTL interface modification
## 3.1 Defect passivation
Similar to the ETL/perovskite interface, the perovskite/HTL interface is also playing a key role in maximizing the photovoltaic performance of PSCs. Major functions of the interface modification as reviewed in the previous section are also effective in perovskite/HTL interface. In particular, the passivation of detrimental defects has been intensively studied with perovskite/
HTL interface modification. While referring to the natural passivation effect of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) in silicon-based solar cells, \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) has also been proven to be useful for natural passivation of the surface of perovskite [112–117]. In 2013, Supasai et al. [118] studied the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) layer after vacuum heating through the modulated surface photovoltage spectroscopy (SPV) and verified the existence of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) on the surface of perovskite layer. Since then, the passivation mechanism of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) enriched the perovskite surface after annealing was reasonably proposed by Chen et al. [116]. At the ETL/perovskite interface, The presence of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) can reduce the non-radiative recombination of predominant defects \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) sites from \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and holes. And at the perovskite/HTL interface, the formation of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) can shifts the conduction band orientation upward, thereby increasing the difficulty of recombination between the electrons from perovskite layer and holes from HTL (as shown in Fig. 7(a)). To reveal the correlation between the ratio of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)and their passivation effects, Chen et al. [119] studied the number of defect densities upon the passivation by different ratios of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)using femtosecond transient absorption spectroscopy. It showed that the perovskite defect density decreased the most with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\): \(\mathrm{I}^{-}=1:2.57\), and the associated carrier lifetime is extended to 2.19 times, which is the best passivation effect reported to date. Besides, alkylammonium halides have also been found to have a comprehensive passivation effect both on the anion and cation vacancies at the interface, and more reliable passivation results can be obtained through hydrogen bonding or ionic bonding [120–124]. By spin coating phenylethylammonium iodide/methylammonium iodide (PEAI/MAI) and n-butylammonium iodide/methylammonium iodide (BAI/MAI) dissolved in acetone, PEAI and BAI were introduced by Hu et al. [125] in 2016 for the interface passivation of perovskite films. The formation of a perovskite double-layer can be observed with the aid of grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS). Due to the ordered 2D perovskite passivation layer formed by PEAI (as shown in Fig. 7(b)), a better passivation effect than BAI was obtained, achieving a PCE of up to \(16.84\%\). Inspired by this work, a hybrid passivation treatment was proposed by Cho et al. [126], where a combination of formamimidinium iodide (FAI) and isobutyl iodide (i-BAI) were used to design the interface modification layer (as shown in Fig 7(c)). I-BAI reacted with the residual \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in the perovskite precursor solution, forming a dense crystalline passivation layer and eliminating surface defects of the perovskite, and consequently the significant improvement in PCE, \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF. Due to the insulating properties of conventional conjugated ligands, the interfacial carrier transport capability of perovskite films is often weakened when the interface defects are passivated. In order to overcome this contradiction, Wu et al. [127] introduced a functional organic amine salt 3-phenyl-2-propene-1-amine iodide (PPEAI) to modify the interfacial characteristics of perovskite films via the "interfacial stitching effect". It can effectively passivate the surface interstitial defects \((X_{\mathrm{i}}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{i}})\) and vacancies \((V_{\mathrm{X}})\) by ammonium functional group and iodide, reducing the non-radiative recombination at the interface. At the same time, improved carrier-transport was observed in the modified film (as shown in Fig. 7(d)) due to the unique \(\pi\)-conjugation and delocalization property of PPEAI. In a recent work, n-hexyltrimethyl ammonium bromide (n-HABr) has been used to passivate defects of the perovskite film by Yang et al. [128]. Interestingly, it was found the passivation not only take effect on the upper surface of the perovskite film, but also the surface at the bottom by establishing an in-situ spectroscopy techniques to study the microstructure–property relations at the buried interface, which provides a new way for in-depth
analysis of device performance loss and modification mechanism. Besides, it was found that Lewis acid-bases can form complex with defects without electron transfer. Lewis acid can accept donor electron pairs, which is often used to passivate negatively charged defects with lone pair electrons [129–132]. On the contrary, Lewis bases have the ability to donate electron pairs so that effectively passivate positively charged defects [132–138]. Lewis acid (e.g., alkali metal chlorides) was reported by Chen et al. [139] as an interface modification layer (as shown in Fig. 7(e)). Experimental and theoretical results showed that the alkali metal chloride interface modification improves the orderly arrangement of the perovskite film, reducing the density of surface defects and interface rearrangement. This ultimately resulted in an increase in \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) from 1.07 to \(1.15\;\mathrm{eV}.\) Similarly, Zheng et al. [140] comprehensively used synergistic combination strategies (including post-annealing, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) -plasma, and potassium chloride treatments) to modify the magnetron sputtered \(\mathrm{NiO}_{x}\) surface, which remarkably improved the charge extraction of the interface, increased the conductivity of the \(\mathrm{NiO}_{x}\) layer, and reduced the interface defect density. At about the same time, the benefits of Lewis base materials such as thiophene and pyridine as the interlayer were also reported by Neol et al. [141]. The PCE of the passivated device increased by \(17.7\%\) and \(26.7\%\) , respectively, which was attributed to the effective passivation of thiophene and pyridine combined with the insufficiently coordinated \(\mathrm{Pb^{+}}\) ions. In a recent study by Wang et al. [142], three types of phosphorus-containing Lewis acid and base molecules (triphenylphosphine oxide (TPPO), tetraisopropyl methylenediphosphonate (TMPP) and
tris(pentafluorophenyl) phosphine (TPFP)) were used as an interface modification layer. It is proposed that TPPO and TMPP can bind to the positively charged undercoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) interstitial defects \((\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{i}})\), or X-site vacancies \((\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{x}})\) as electron donors (as shown in Fig 7(f)). And a stronger positive charge center is formed at the centered phosphorus atom in TPFP. As a result, it can not only reduce recombination and the density of surface traps, but also suppress phase segregation, leading to a champion PCE of \(22.02\%\). Compared with the modification on ETL/perovskite interface of n-i-p devices, there are apparently less limitation of materials on perovskite/ ETL interface. Passivation layer can be even simultaneously formed with the perovskite layer when excess inorganic salts. In our previous studies aimed at replacing lead with alkaline-earth metals, it has been surprisingly found that the Sr and Ca cations segregated at the surface of perovskite film forming a Sr-rich and Ca-rich interlayers instead of replacing the B-site Pb ions. Such interlayers contribute to a higher Voc compared to the pristine device, and therefore obtaining improved device performance [143, 144]. Another effective inorganic modification layer was recently reported by Yang et al. [145], where octylammonium sulfate was spin-coated on the perovskite film and formed a compact inorganic lead oxysalt layer on the surface through in situ reaction with lead halide perovskite. The wide-bandgap lead oxysalt layers can reduce the defect-nucleating sites on the perovskite surfaces by passivating undercoordinated surface lead ions. Such inorganic modified layer exhibits superior stability, which is highly desired for the development of commercial PSC devices in the future.
## 3.2 Inhibiting ion migration
3.2 Inhibiting ion migrationThe modification on HTL/perovskite interface is equally important as ETL/perovskite interface in terms of inhibiting the ion migration and keep the charge transport layer away from the impact of mobile ions. For instance, the accumulation of \(\Gamma\) ions at the perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD interface lead to formation of the neutral iodine derivative Spiro-OMeTAD-I or other side products. The reaction would be irreversible, therefore causing permanent damage to the device performance in both PCE and stability [146]. It is reported by Han et al. [147] that the migration of \(\Gamma\) ions would disorder the favorable semiconductor heterostructure formed by perovskites and HTL. After introducing a chlorinated graphene oxide (Cl-GO) layer between perovskite and Spiro-OMeTAD, the migration of \(\Gamma\) is found less significant benefiting form strong Pb-Cl and Pb-O bonds at the Pb-rich surface of perovskite. Cross-sectional characterization by ToF-SIMS (as shown in Fig. 8(a)) shows the HTL in the aged control device was already fully occupied by \(\Gamma\), whereas only a low signal can be observed from the aged perovskite/ Cl-GO sample. Subsequently, Ma et al. [148] presented \(\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}\) modification to increase the barrier energy for mobile ions, and thereby achieving the function on fixing halide ions in perovskite lattice. Furthermore, in the post-treatment of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) with excess \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) by PEAI, Liu et al. [149] found that it could convert \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{PBA}_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{4}\) precisely at the grain boundary and surface. 2D perovskite formed in situ can not only passivate the defects on the surface and block the moisture but also act as a barrier to inhibit ion diffusion, effectively reducing the concentration of migrated \(\Gamma\) in the HTL and \(\mathrm{Ag}\) electrode layers (as shown in Fig. 8(b)). In addition to the diffusion of halogen ions, the diffusion of organic cations such as \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) in perovskite will cause decomposition and degradation of perovskite and HTL, which also damage the long-term stability of device performance. The importance of preventing \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) ion migration into organic HTL was studied by Zhao et al. [150] with a spin-coated CuSCN layer on the surface of perovskite. From the XPS, a peak with \(401.7\;\mathrm{eV}\) center position which can be allocated to methylamine appeared in the control device, while no signal exceeding \(401\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be found in the
modified device (as shown in Figs. 8(c) and 8(d)), which strongly suggests that CuSCN acted as an effective blocking layer for \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) ion migration. On this basis, cesium acetate, as a buffer layer into PSCs was incorporated by Zhao et al. [151] to reduce ion migration. On one hand, cesium acetate film would react with perovskite to form a Cs-rich perovskite at the surface, and the high Cs ratio at interface mitigates the organic cation migration (such as \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\)). On the other hand, residue cesium acetate tended to consume these unblocked mobile ions at the interface via an in-situ reaction (as shown in Fig. 8(e)). The synergistic effect of the cesium acetate made the modified PSC maintain a high PCE (\(>20\%\)) with improved stability. From the above studies, building an ion barrier at perovskite/HTL interface is obviously going to be an effective strategy to fabricate stable PSCs.
## 3.3 Band-level alignment
3.3 Band-level alignmentOptimizing the energy level alignment between the perovskite and HTL is beneficial to the hole extraction and the achievement of higher \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\). Generally, because the organic modified material deposited after the perovskite will not be dissolved, researchers have been focusing on organic compounds that are easier to achieve precise control of energy levels than inorganic materials [152–154]. A novel small organic molecule consisting of triphenylamine (TPA) and amino functional groups were introduced in recent study by Zhao et al. [155] where these organic molecules were spin-coated on the surface of the perovskite as an interface buffer layer. It was found that the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level of TPA derivatives is about \(-5.4\sim-5.5\;\mathrm{eV}\), which is between perovskite and HTL, resulting an improvement in matching the energy level of perovskite and HTL (as shown in Fig. 9(a)). In a recent study by Salado et al. [156], three types of thiazoline iodide (TMI) were used as a hydrophobic interface modification layer. The highest occupied molecular orbital-lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (HOMO-LUMO) levels of these three salts are suitable for charge extraction, showing a large HOMO-LUMO energy gap, which can effectively extract holes from the perovskite layer. At the same time, the high LUMO can act as
an electronic barrier between the anode and the perovskite layer (as shown in Fig. 9(b)). Meanwhile, Gong et al. [157] used different nanocrystals (NCs) with the same organic capping ligand as the interface modification layer, resulting in the highest average PCE of \(21.5\%\). This performance enhancement is attributed to the optimum alignment of the cascade bands at the interface between the perovskite film and the NC layer (as shown in Fig. 9(c)). A more recent report by Tan et al. [158] showed that reasonable interface modification employing an organic semiconductor of PTPD flattens the energy extraction (as shown in Fig. 9(d)) while effectively passivating the back contact, therefore prohibited interface carrier recombination and obtained a decent photovoltaic performance with FF up to \(83\%\). Subsequent report by Zhu et al. [159] also indicated the
effectiveness of 4-tert-butyl-benzyl ammonium iodide (tBBAI). PSC with tBBAI interface layer showed stronger charge transfer than the control devices. This might be related to a better contact between the highly polarized perovskite layer and the non-polarized HTL, which reduced the hole extraction barrier. In addition to inserting materials with an appropriate energy gap as the interface modification layer to optimize the energy level matching, it is also feasible to directly modify the energy level of the perovskite layer through interface modification. Gao et al. [160] spin-coated 1-naphthylmethylamine iodide (NMAI) with a large volume to passivate the perovskite surface. The interaction between NMAI and the dominant negatively-charged-ion defects leads to the formation of dipoles, which makes the energy level of the perovskite surface
bending upward and thus closer to that of Spiro-OMeTAD. It is conducive to hole collection and alignment due to the improved energy band matching (as shown in Figs. 9(e) and 9(f)), resulting in high PCE of above \(21\%\). A similar result was reported by Lim et al. [161] in p-i-n PSCs. They incorporated perfluorinated ionomer (PFI) into PEDOT: PSS to form a self-organized hole extraction layer (SOHEL) rich in PFI. Through this interface modification strategy, the interface work function was increased to \(-5.4~\mathrm{eV}\) (as shown in Fig. 9(g)), which dramatically increased the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of the device by 0.153V. Perovskite quantum dots (QDs) are also widely used in band-level alignment due to their high absorption coefficient and tunable energy level. Cha et al. [162] developed an interface engineering method based on perovskite QDs, which effectively promote the hole transfer from perovskite film to HTL. A series of organometallic halide perovskite QDs with various Br:I ratios were synthesized and deposited between the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite and HTL as an interface modification, the VB edge of \(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3-\mathrm{I}_{x}}\) QDs has been just located between the VB edge of the \(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3-\mathrm{I}_{x}}\) perovskite film and the HOMO lever of the HTL layer (as shown in Fig. 9(h)), resulting in significant improvements in the FF, short-circuit photocurrent, and PCE. The interface modification by perovskite QDs were also applied in all-inorganic PSCs by Bian et al. [163]. Graded bandgap structure was formed in PSCs after \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) substitution, \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\) capping and \(\left[\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)_{2}\mathrm{CH}\right]^{+}\) treatment (as shown in Fig. 9(i)), optimizing the band-level alignment which improve carrier transport between the functional layer and showing a \(\mathrm{PCE}\%\) of \(14.45\%\). Another work with a more concise post-processing was reported by Zhang et al. [164]. The dimension-grading heterojunction based on a QDs/bulk film structure was post-treated by soaking in the formamidinium iodide (FAI) ethyl acetate (EA) solution, which provide suitable gradient energy levels and less surface defect density and therefore effectively inhibited the recombination loss and improved the hole extraction. Band-level alignment is also very important for the large-area devices, Qi et al. [165] modified the ETL/perovskite interface by ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid dipotassium salt (EDTAK) combining with the ethylammonium iodide/methylammonium iodide (EAI/MAI) modification at the perovskite/HTL interface to realize an optimized the energy-level alignment (as shown in Fig. 9(j)) for their perovskite modules. Benefiting from a better charge extraction, their modified perovskite solar modules presented an PCE of \(16.6\%\) on a designated area of \(22.4~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), while the unmodified modules only shows \(11.4\%\) with a much lower \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF. Nevertheless, band-matching functional layers usually have very strict requirements on their thickness, raising issues on the processing reproducibility [166, 167]. More challenges remain in the application of large-area devices due to the difficulty of ensuring the uniformity of the modification layer.
## 3.4 Stability improvement
Interface modification plays a more important role in improving the stability of PSCs, which is of critical importance for the future commercialization and deployment of perovskite photovoltaics. The stability issue of PSC is not only related to the ion migration, but also the sensitive nature of organic-inorganic metal halide perovskites to the ambient environment. Various degradation reactions are prone to occur upon exposure to the factors such as moisture, oxygen, light, and high temperature, resulting in the degradation of device performance [168–171]. 2D perovskites have attracted much attention recently because of its intrinsic stability, but there is still a substantial lag in PCE compared to 3D perovskites [172–177]. Meanwhile, the
application of 2D perovskite as an interface modification in n-i-p devices showed much success in improving the moisture stability of perovskite thin films and devices [178-181]. Depositing alkyl halides on the surface of the perovskite is a typical method for forming 2D perovskite layers [182–185]. Two new materials, TCPBr and TCPI, were demonstrated by Ma et al. [186] to effectively modify the interface. Since it has ethylene, \(\mathrm{TCP}^{+}\) can act as a hydrophobic interface modification layer to significantly inhibit moisture penetration and ion migration, thereby greatly reducing the hysteresis and improving device stability (as shown in Figs. 10(a) and 10(b)). After storage for more than \(1{,}000\;\mathrm{h}\), unencapsulated solar cells with TCPBr treatment can still retain \(90\%\) of its initial PCE. Similarly, the self-crystallization of multifunctional 2D perovskite \(\mathrm{BnA}_{2}\mathrm{ZPb}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{7}\) has been confirmed by Kim et al. [189], where the perovskite/ HTL interface was modified with benzyl ammonium iodide (BnAI) by spin-coating. It can be found that the change of additive concentration affects the crystal orientation of the interface modification layer (as shown in Fig. 10(c)). Generally, horizontally orientated 2D perovskites can impede efficient hole transport, while randomly oriented 2D perovskite can facilitate hole transport by increasing the possibility of contact between adjacent inorganic slabs. Recently, comparative studies on different 2D perovskites have been carried out to analyze the effect of their chemical structure on device performance and stability. In a recent study by Seok et al. [120], they systematically studied the influence of alkyl chain length of butylammonium iodide (BAI), octylammonium iodide (OAI), and dodecylammonium iodide (DAI) on the interface modification. Experiments showed that with the increase in alkyl chain length, the ability to block electrons and moisture resistance of the interface modification layer can be significantly enhanced (as shown in Figs. 10(d)–10(f)). Interestingly, Liu et al. [188] considered the influence of hydrophobicity, dipole moment and passivation effect of different 2D perovskites on the interface carrier transport properties and device stability. By spin-coating 4-Chlorobenzylammonium iodide (CBAI) and 4-Bromobenzylammonium iodide (BBAI) as capping layers, the surface morphology on the top of 3D perovskite film turned to be dense and smooth, leading to less defective interface with suppressed nonradiative recombination and moisture invasion for boosted device performance and long-term stability. In another work [189], it systematically studied the effect of the 2D perovskite interface layer formed by five kinds of ammonium halides (2-methoxyethylammonium iodide, phenethylammonium iodide, p-methyl-phenethylammonium iodide, p-methoxyphenethylammonium iodide, p-nitrophenethylammonium iodide) and further confirmed that the positive role of 2D perovskite layer in device performance mainly lies in the interface modification rather than doping. Due to the limitation of process technology (i.e., the organic solvent used when preparing 3D perovskite will dissolve 2D perovskite), 2D perovskite modification layer is usually reported to modify the upper surface of the perovskite instead of the lower surface. Recently, based on a single-sided 2D perovskite interface modification layer, White et al. [190] introduced n-BAI to modify both of the ETL/perovskite layer and the perovskite layer/HTL interface. Surprisingly, device FF was improved despite that the 2D perovskite interface modification layer is semi-insulating. This phenomenon is attributed to the fact that the incomplete coverage of the 2D perovskite layer allows effective localized passivation through non-passivated areas such as pinholes, thus ensuring effective charge transfer across the device interface. Except for 2D perovskites, there are hydrophobic materials that also can be used as interface protective layers. It has been reported that the use of a hydro-
phobic intermediate layer polystyrene (PS) for double-sided modification can improve device performance and stability [191]. PS was first spin-coated on the surface of the \(\mathrm{NiO}_{x}\) film as a modification layer, providing a smooth growth surface to improve the quality of the perovskite film. Then PS mixed in chlorobenzene (CB) was used as an anti-solvent, which can slow down the growth of perovskite crystals and fill the traps between the perovskite grains. The moisture stability of the device is significantly improved under the protection of the double-sided hydrophobic functional layer. Nevertheless, the thermal stability of the organic modification layer remains a huge concern. For example, the passivation effect of the PEAI modified device almost disappears after thermal annealing [25], which limits the stability of its performance under elevated temperature in real circumstances. In contrast, the inorganic modified layer is considered more promising. The exfoliated \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) flakes introduced by Capasso et al. [192] act as a protective interfacial modification layer between the perovskite/ HTL. The formation of shunt contacts between the perovskite and the Au electrode can be prevented by the \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) modification. Compared with the reference devices without \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\), the modified devices achieved a higher lifetime stability over \(550\,\mathrm{h}\) (\(\Delta\mathrm{PCE}/\mathrm{PCE}=-7\%\) vs. \(\Delta\mathrm{PCE}/\mathrm{PCE}=-34\%\)). In the work of Yang et al. [145], the surface of perovskite is converted into water-insoluble lead (II) oxysalt through reaction with octylammonium sulfate. These surface-covered lead-oxygen salts not only provide favorable hydrophobicity for the perovskite film and inhibit ion migration by immobilizing surface defects such as vacancies, but also afford much better resistance to many detrimental stimuli under ambient atmosphere and light irradiation by forming strong chemical bonds. Although significant progress has been achieved in the current research on the stability of PSCs, these works have paid more attention on the moisture stability, and ignored the illumination and thermal cycle stress stability which are considered to be more critical and unavoidable factors during the operation of the device. Relative to the significant improvement in PCE, the current stability improvement is still far from meeting industry standards, which will be the next key breakthrough for PSCs.
## 3.5 Morphology control
HTL/perovskite interface modification also has the effect of
improving the morphology of perovskite. Many approaches have been reported to optimize the morphology of the perovskite film by providing a desired substrate with excellent wettability [193], smaller roughness [194, 195], to reduce defect sites [196] as well as the internal stress during the crystallization [197]. Such modifications can be often found in p-i-n devices, where the perovskite is deposited on the modified HTL [198]. Luo et al. [193] tried spi-coating graphene-oxide (GO) onto the surface of Poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene): poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS) to form an interface modification layer, with the modified device showing significantly improved stability. This beneficial effect can be attributed not only to the removal of the moisture-absorbing PSS material on the HTL surface after GO modification, but also to the excellent wettability of the GO modified layer helps to form a high-quality perovskite layer (as shown in Figs. 11(a) and 11(b)). Duan et al. [194] also proved that ideal growth conditions have a benign effect on the morphology of perovskite films. In their experiment, different metal oxide \(\left(\mathrm{A}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}\right)\) was used as the interface modification layer. As seen in the AFM and SEM (as shown in Fig. 11(c)), the perovskite crystal has the best quality on the \(\mathrm{MoO}_{x}\) surface (the largest and most uniform grains), because the \(\mathrm{MoO}_{x}\) film with small roughness and excellent wettability is conducive to the growth of perovskite crystal. In addition, the special effects of RbCl on the HTL/perovskite interface were studied by Liu et al. [195]. Because RbCl crystallite possesses similar polyhedral structure and lattice parameters as the perovskite (as shown in Figs. 11(d) and 11(e)), which is beneficial for the seed-mediated growth of perovskite, it ultimately helps to form a dense and uniform active layer with superior crystallinity and less trap density. Except for growth conditions, there are other factors that also can improve perovskite morphology. It has been reported that nitrogen and oxygen atoms in methoxy-functionalized triphenylamine-imidazole derivatives (TPI-MEO) can reduce defect sites and improve perovskite crystallinity through forming Lewis adducts with under coordinated Pb ions in perovskite [196]. Xie et al. [197] found that the presence of adenine as an intermediate layer can prevent direct interaction between \(\mathrm{NiO}_{x}\) and perovskite layer, and reduce the bond length of Pb-I between the first layer (surface layer) and the second layer (the nearest layer) of perovskite to \(\sim3.13\) from \(\sim3.7\%\) , which is helpful in
keeping perovskite in its crystal structure. Considering the leading position of p-i-n devices in the pilot scale manufacturing of PSCs, the relevant interface engineering will also play an important role in the commercialization of PSCs.
## 4 Summary and outlook
With the development of PSCs and in-depth research of perovskite photovoltaics, interface engineering has been shown essential in the design and fabrication of high-performance and stable devices. By reviewing the recent progress of interfacial modification for both ETL/perovskite and HTL/perovskite interfaces, we concluded that the general functions of these novel interface modification can be divided into the following categories: 1) Interfacial defects passivation by employing chemical interaction of specific functional groups to fill vacancies or immobilize interstitial atoms; 2) Improvement of energy band alignment between the adjacent layers by modifying the functional layer interface to facilitate an efficient charge extraction; 3) Optimizing the perovskite growth process to assist the formation of uniform, dense, and high-quality perovskite films; 4) Serving as a barrier layer to improve device stability by blocking external moisture penetration or inhibiting the ion-migration. Although the interface engineering has been demonstrated to improve the device performance and stability in many ways, a broader implementation still encounters various challenges, particularly the reproducibility issues from lab to lab. Moreover, the effectiveness of the interface modification is highly dependent on the deposition techniques and the perovskite components. The effectiveness of these various interfacial modifications on different device architectures still needs to be further studied and verified. Therefore, the underlying mechanisms of interface engineering on perovskite/charge extraction layers from both chemical and physical perspectives need to be further understood.
Despite the fact that the remarkable improvement in device PCE and stability of the PSCs had made it better than organic solar cells so far, the overall durability of the perovskite devices is still far from satisfactory when compared with silicon and other commercialized inorganic thin film photovoltaic technologies. To address the stability issue, further efforts need to be devoted to not only the materials engineering of perovskite itself but also the interface engineering to suppress the ion-migration and moisture infiltration. Meanwhile, stability tests according to relevant IEC standards under actual working environment or stress conditions are also of great significance. It requires more research attention to be paid on the cycling stability of PSCs under illumination or thermal cycling stress as well as the combined illumination and thermal stress, where the interfacial modification is considered to be more critical in these scenarios. The device physics and photochemical process with interface engineering should be better understood to guide the device design for future PSCs and modules.
Last but not least, interface engineering of PSCs has shown their necessity in obtaining high performance as well as long-lasting durability, which is considered to be an important process for realizing the future commercialization of PSCs. To promote the incorporation of these interface engineering processes into upscalable manufacturing, the feasibility and cost of the interfacial modifications will also be increasingly critical. Although some of the pioneering works have been reported, the exploration of the interfacial engineering on large area perovskite devices and modules are still fairly limited in such a hot area. The majority of the approaches have only demonstrated their effectiveness on small-area devices. Their effectiveness and application on large-area devices needs more in-depth research. In this regard, more efforts should be paid to develop low-cost and robust interface modification materials and methods to meet the demand of commercialization in the next stage. With the help of qualified interface
engineering, the potential of perovskite photovoltaics will be further tapped and the PCE of PSCs will continuously step forward towards its Shockley–Queisser limit. It is also foreseeable that the successful application of interface engineering on large-area devices will contribute to a great leap in the future commercialization of perovskite photovoltaics.
